00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.240
For those of you afraid of spiders, the&nbsp;
microcosmos probably seems like a safe haven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.160
When you’re scanning through a microscopic&nbsp;
ocean full of single-celled organisms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.223
the likelihood of a spider crawling&nbsp;
across your screen feels comfortingly low.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.097 --> 00:00:55.154
The few times we’ve seen a spider under the&nbsp;
microscope, it’s because we put it there.&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.840
But safe as the microcosmos might&nbsp;
seem from a surprise spider attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.680
it would be misleading to pretend that it’s&nbsp;
completely free of spider-like sightings. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.937
even at this small scale, you could find yourself&nbsp;
subject to an ambush of the arachnid sort.&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.560
These are water mites, relatives of spiders&nbsp;
in that they are also members of the Arachnid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:29.234
class. And the resemblance is uncanny,&nbsp;
especially with the eight legs waving around.&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.960
But that detail aside, there is
something about this water mite that looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.080
more like a cartoon spider, something&nbsp;
you might have doodled in a notebook.&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:49.280
For one, there’s the eyes, those simple dots.&nbsp;
Water mite species usually have two pairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.480
which act mainly to detect light. It’s much&nbsp;
less intimidating to stare into the eyes of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:01.200
water mite than into the eight eyes of a spider.
Another reason for the cartoon spider-iness of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.600
some water mites is down to a defining aspect&nbsp;
of their shape. Spiders have segmented bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.920
with an abdomen that is distinct from&nbsp;
the cephalothorax that holds its head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.383
But water mites aren’t segmented. Instead,&nbsp;
those different parts are all fused together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.720
In the case of this particular species, the&nbsp;
final result is round and kind of adorable.&nbsp;

00:02:26.411 --> 00:02:31.600
So now we’re going to disrupt that cuteness by&nbsp;
bringing up a creature we’ve talked about before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.823
one that’s more closely related to water mites&nbsp;
and one that does not evoke any sense of cuteness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.920
We’re talking about dust mites, which are part of&nbsp;
the same superorder of mites called Acariformes.&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.520
If you haven’t watched our episode about&nbsp;
dust mites before, you should check it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.000
out after watching this video, especially if&nbsp;
you’re looking for motivation to clean your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.600
room. Because without spoiling too much, the main&nbsp;
thing to know about dust mites is that their poop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.440
is, it’s not great.
I’m bringing that up now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.800
because while water mite poop might not evoke&nbsp;
the same concern that dust mite poop does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:13.886
it is still fascinating. Their excretory system&nbsp;
is made of a large tube connected to a pore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.286 --> 00:03:19.520
The tube takes in waste from the mite’s version&nbsp;
of blood, called hemolymph. And in the tube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.120
the waste is stored as yellow or white&nbsp;
crystals. To clear out its storage of crystals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.251
the mite simply moves its muscles to push&nbsp;
everything out through its excretory pore.&nbsp;

00:03:30.811 --> 00:03:34.800
From the studies we’ve found on other mites&nbsp;
with similar systems, our best guess is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:41.029
these poops are guanine crystals, produced by&nbsp;
the mite’s own body as it breaks down nutrients.&nbsp;

00:03:41.337 --> 00:03:47.040
Now crystal poop is weird. But the weirder 
thing&nbsp;is that crystaline poop is not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.560
weirdest thing that water mites excrete.
But before we get there, let’s talk about just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:59.097
red some of our water mites are. They’re so red&nbsp;
that they seem like walking drops of blood.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.480
They are not actually chock-full of blood though;
 the red&nbsp;color is likely just some kind of carotenoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.720
that turns the water mite crimson.
And for a time, scientists believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.000
that the red color was a way for&nbsp;
these species of water mites to flash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.297
a warning signal to fish, a sort of “Do not eat&nbsp;
me” expressed solely through threatening hue.&nbsp;

00:04:20.903 --> 00:04:26.880
But like why? Why did scientists think fish would&nbsp;
heed this warning anyway? Why would a fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.851
bother to listen to a water mite&nbsp;
when it could be eating instead?&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.960
Well, many water mite species have an&nbsp;
approach to warding off predators that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:46.640
is…sticky. Water mites have little globe-shaped&nbsp;
organs dotting their bodies called glandularia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.360
each with their own little opening and a little&nbsp;
bristle-y hair called a seta. When something, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.040
a fish, brushes up against that hair, it triggers&nbsp;
the glandularia into releasing a milky fluid into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.880
the water, where it mixes into a tackier,&nbsp;
more viscous substance. And for predators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.600
the idea of having to wade through some gloppy&nbsp;
mess to get to your food is probably unappealing.&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.903
So when the scientists saw, in the context of their own&nbsp;
experiments, that fish seemed to quickly

00:05:14.903 --> 00:05:19.280
stop trying to eat water mites, they decided&nbsp;
that the red coloring was the water mites’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:25.680
way of telling the fish, “We’re the sticky prey.”
But while it’s possible the fish do learn not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.600
pick out these water mites for food,&nbsp;
the idea that the red color exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.600
specifically as a warning to predators does not&nbsp;
hold up to the basics of how these particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.440
species live. Red water mite species tend&nbsp;
to be found in transient pools of water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.240
places that fish don’t often find themselves.&nbsp;
So the likelihood that they would have evolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:52.103
this trait for the purpose of warning fish is low.&nbsp;
It’s more likely that the pigments are a way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.103 --> 00:05:58.297
protecting them from ultraviolet light. It just&nbsp;maybe
happened to also protect them from fish as well.&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:05.006
And the sticky fluid that makes them unappealing to&nbsp;
fish also has another use: reproduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.000
In one genus called Arrenurus, the males use it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.200
hold the females still during copulation.
This is part of a wider spectrum of weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.080
reproductive behaviors. On one end, there are&nbsp;
the species like the ones we just mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.920
which engage very directly and&nbsp;
physically with each other.&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.280
On the other end of the spectrum, there are water&nbsp;
mite species where the sexes don’t feel the need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:37.760
to interact at all to make reproduction happen.&nbsp;
They don’t communicate, they don’t touch. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:44.240
males simply deposit spermatophores somewhere for&nbsp;
their female counterparts to find them and pick them up.&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:48.720
And then, somewhere between the directly&nbsp;
copulating water mites and the cold shoulder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.520
water mites, there are the dancers. They’re&nbsp;
sort of like the second group in that the males&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.800
drop off their spermatophores. But then they&nbsp;
actually stick around, mostly so they can draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:05.120
females in and help them find the spermatophores.
And this is where dancing comes in. Sometimes this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:08.480
involves vibrating their legs&nbsp;
to mimic their favorite prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:14.080
drawing the attention (and aggression) of a&nbsp;
wandering female. Other times though, this means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:20.720
waltzing together as a pair, moving in circles&nbsp;
with careful pauses to pick up the spermatophores.&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.520
We obviously don’t have video of that or&nbsp;
we would be showing it to you. But at some point,&nbsp;

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:31.520
some scientist watched this happen. Someone&nbsp;
watched the dance of the mating water mites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.400
and then they carefully documented it.&nbsp;
Just like someone saw the mite-averse fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:43.269
and the crystal poops, and the many other strange&nbsp;
things that make up the lives of water mites.

